73},{"active_advertisers":2,"ad_ctr":0.009824781574510976,"ad_revenue":510730.33,"advertisers__account_tier":"mid_market","advertisers__industry":"auto","arpa":255365.165,"conversions":27049,"cpa":18.88167141114274,"roas":4.643172846225913},{"active_advertisers":14,"ad_ctr":0.008394859873056383,"ad_revenue":440274.66,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"retail","arpa":31448.19,"conversions":19149,"cpa":22.99204449318502,"roas":3.493608217197874},{"active_advertisers":9,"ad_ctr":0.00902545773620978,"ad_revenue":430381.61,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"entertainment","arpa":47820.17888888888,"conversions":19818,"cpa":21.716702492683417,"roas":3.7899930250272544},{"active_advertisers":12,"ad_ctr":0.006770837008621306,"ad_revenue":403977.34,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"beauty","arpa":33664.778333333335,"conversions":8662,"cpa":46.6378827060725,"roas":1.6823788433281923},{"active_advertisers":13,"ad_ctr":0.008507231053914793,"ad_revenue":400409.68,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"finance","arpa":30800.744615384618,"conversions":18549,"cpa":21.586591190899775,"roas":3.746285104795669},{"active_advertisers":12,"ad_ctr":0.00795344366463844,"ad_revenue":397187.21,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"travel","arpa":33098.934166666666,"conversions":16965,"cpa":23.41215502505158,"roas":3.4586394159066702},{"active_advertisers":8,"ad_ctr":0.00727529073749764,"ad_revenue":195535.85,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"cpg","arpa":24441.98125,"conversions":5362,"cpa":36.46696195449459,"roas":2.2621288116731537},{"active_advertisers":7,"ad_ctr":0.008021421653331725,"ad_revenue":187741.31,"advertisers__account_tier":"self_serve","advertisers__industry":"auto","arpa":26820.187142857143,"conversions":6036,"cpa":31.103596752816433,"roas":2.65230166978168}]}]}],"answer":"I split advertisers into segments by account tier and industry. Revenue is very concentrated: the 25 managed advertisers (17% of 148) brought in 81.7% of the $55.7M in ad revenue from Oct 2025 to Sep 2026 [1][3].\n\n| Tier | Revenue | Advertisers | Share of revenue | Revenue per advertiser | ROAS |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Managed | $45.5M | 25 | 81.7% | $1.82M | 3.64 |\n| Mid-market | $7.2M | 35 | 12.9% | $205K | 3.52 |\n| Self-serve | $3.0M | 88 | 5.4% | $34K | 2.95 |\n\n- **Where the money is:** Managed advertisers in entertainment ($11.1M), retail ($10.5M) and travel ($8.8M) bring in 54% of all revenue [1].\n- **Most at risk of leaving:** Self-serve beauty (ROAS 1.68, cost per conversion $46.64), self-serve home furnishing (2.18, $37.89) and self-serve CPG (2.26, $36.47). These are the weakest segments, so they are the most likely to stop spending [1].\n- **Strongest results:** Auto has the best ROAS in managed (4.67) and mid-market (4.64), and mid-market CPG reaches 4.01 [1]. Advertisers in these groups are good candidates to move up a tier or grow their budgets.\n- **Room to grow:** Mid-market advertisers get returns close to managed (3.52 vs 3.64) but spend about 9x less each [2]. Moving some of them to managed accounts may be the easiest way to add revenue.\n\nThe chart [4] plots every segment by revenue per advertiser and ROAS.\n\nCaveats: several managed segments have only 1 or 2 advertisers, so one account can swing their numbers. ROAS uses the conversion values advertisers report themselves. This splits advertisers by their assigned tier and industry, not by statistical clustering. I didn't look at user (consumer) segments.","model":"claude-opus-5-5","role":"analyst","at":"2026-10-11T20:14:48.039Z","secs":20,"usage":{"in":11676,"out":2065,"cache":5980}}]}